---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base title="install · keel" description="Installing keel under Claude Code takes two lines. Setup is one conversation, removal is one command, and Node is the only hard requirement.">
  <header class="pagehead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">install</span>
      <h2>Two lines in, one conversation to configure, one command out</h2>
      <p class="sub">Removal gets the same documentation as installation. If you can't
      turn a tool off, sooner or later you'll resent it.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="step">
        <div class="n">1</div>
        <div>
          <h3>Install the plugin</h3>
          <p>Run these inside Claude Code. They're the same two lines on a fresh setup or
          a years-old one. A plugin install only adds things, so your existing
          configuration stays as it is.</p>
          <pre><code><span class="p">/plugin</span> marketplace add redlinelabs-dev/keel
<span class="p">/plugin</span> install keel@keel</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <div class="n">2</div>
        <div>
          <h3>Let Claude walk you through configuration</h3>
          <p>Start a fresh session. Claude checks what's already set up and asks only
          about what's missing. That can be a name for this machine, where your notebook
          lives, your projects, and the recommended safety settings. It runs the
          commands itself. You can run it again any time as a check-up.</p>
          <pre><code><span class="p">/keel:setup</span></code></pre>
          <p>Rather do it by hand? Run <code>keel setup --memory-home ~/notes</code>,
          then <code>claude plugin install keel-memory@keel</code>. The notes engine
          needs <a href="https://github.com/astral-sh/uv"><code>uv</code></a>, and the
          next fresh session picks it up.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <div class="n">3</div>
        <div>
          <h3>There is no step three</h3>
          <p>There's no launcher, wrapper, or alias. You start <code>claude</code> the
          same way you would without keel, and keel loads into that plain session.
          <code>keel update</code> pulls the latest version whenever you want. It checks
          the commit it landed on, because a version number can lie.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">requirements</span>
      <h2>Node, and not much else</h2>
      <table>
        <tr><td>node</td><td>The hooks are plain <code>.mjs</code> files with <strong>no
        dependencies</strong>. Your machines might not have bun, deno, python, or jq, but
        they'll have node.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>uv + python ≥ 3.12</td><td>You only need these if you turn on the
        <code>keel-memory</code> adapter. <code>uv</code> installs the right Python for
        you, which is why Basic Memory recommends it.</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">the exits</span>
      <h2>Leaving is one command, and it's documented</h2>
      <table>
        <tr><td>KEEL_GUARD_OFF=1</td><td>Turns the security guard off completely.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>KEEL_ACTIVITY_OFF=1</td><td>Stops the work diary without uninstalling.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>KEEL_TRAILERS_OFF=1</td><td>Turns off the commit signature guard.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>KEEL_INGEST_OFF=1</td><td>Stops stamping web and app content as untrusted.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>KEEL_RECALL_OFF=1</td><td>Stops keel adding memory files to your prompts.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>~/.config/keel/policy.json</td><td>Your own policy. It can add rules, or
        <strong>exempt</strong> any rule keel ships with <code>bash.allow</code> or
        <code>paths.allow</code>. keel checks your exemptions first, and they win.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>/plugin uninstall keel@keel</td><td>Removes the software. Your work diary,
        notes, and backups stay, because they're yours.
        <a href="https://github.com/redlinelabs-dev/keel/blob/main/docs/UNINSTALL.md">UNINSTALL.md</a>
        lists every path in case you want those gone too.</td></tr>
      </table>

      <div class="note"><strong>The fresh start</strong> is the one step that stays a
      script a person runs on purpose. It resets a cluttered config to a clean one with
      keel on top. <code>bash scripts/install.sh</code> walks through it. It never runs
      <code>sudo</code> and never deletes anything. The reset is a <code>mv</code>, and
      the script prints the undo command first. Afterwards, <code>keel migrate</code>
      can bring back marketplaces and plugins from the old config, one at a time or all
      together.</div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
